A Southern classic made famous by Ree Drummond (The Pioneer Woman). Double-breaded tender cube steaks fried until shatteringly crisp and served alongside creamy mashed potatoes under a blanket of scratch skillet pepper gravy.

*Adapted from [Ree Drummond / Food Network](https://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/ree-drummond/chicken-fried-steak-with-gravy-recipe).*

## Ingredients

### Steaks & Breading
- 3 pounds cube steak (extra-tenderized round steak)
- Kosher salt and freshly ground black pepper, to taste
- 1 ½ cups whole milk
- 2 large eggs
- 2 cups all-purpose flour
- 2 teaspoons seasoned salt (such as Lawry's)
- 1 ½ teaspoons freshly ground black pepper
- ¾ teaspoon paprika
- ¼ teaspoon cayenne pepper
- ½ cup canola or vegetable oil
- 1 tablespoon butter

### Skillet Pepper Gravy
- ⅓ cup all-purpose flour
- 3 to 4 cups whole milk
- ½ teaspoon seasoned salt
- Freshly ground black pepper, to taste

### For Serving
- Warm homemade mashed potatoes

## Instructions

1. **Establish the Dredging Station:** Set up a clean workstation with three wide, shallow pie pans or baking dishes:
   - Dish 1: Beat together the 1 ½ cups milk and 2 eggs until smooth.
   - Dish 2: Whisk the 2 cups flour with 2 teaspoons seasoned salt, 1 ½ teaspoons black pepper, paprika, and cayenne pepper.
   - Dish 3: The cube steaks, seasoned lightly on both sides with kosher salt and black pepper.
   - Keep an empty rimmed platter at the end of the line for holding breaded steaks.
2. **Double-Coat the Steaks:** Take one seasoned cube steak at a time and dredge it in the flour mixture to dust all surfaces. Plunge into the egg wash, allowing excess to drip back into the bowl, then return to the flour mixture, firmly pressing the flour into the textured crevices of the meat (dry -> wet -> dry). Set aside on the platter and repeat with all steaks.
3. **Pan-Fry to Deep Golden:** In a heavy 12-inch cast-iron skillet, heat the ½ cup vegetable oil and 1 tablespoon butter over medium heat. When a pinch of flour sizzles immediately upon hitting the pan, add steaks in batches of 2 or 3 without crowding. Fry for about 2 minutes on the first side until golden brown, flip gently with tongs, and fry for 2 minutes on the second side. Move cooked steaks to a paper towel-lined plate and tent with aluminum foil to retain crispness and warmth.
4. **Build the Skillet Roux:** Carefully pour the frying oil out of the skillet into a heat-resistant container, leaving about ¼ cup of hot grease in the skillet along with all the savory browned crumbles. Lower heat to medium-low. Scatter the ⅓ cup flour into the grease and whisk continuously for 1 to 2 minutes until it cooks into a fragrant, toasted roux.
5. **Simmer the Country Cream Gravy:** Gradually stream 3 to 4 cups of milk into the roux while whisking vigorously to prevent lumps. Season with ½ teaspoon seasoned salt and an abundant grind of black pepper. Simmer gently, stirring across the bottom and sides, for 5 to 10 minutes until the gravy turns creamy and velvety. If it gets too thick, whisk in a splash more milk.
6. **Plate and Serve:** Dish up the hot, crunchy steaks alongside scoops of buttery mashed potatoes. Ladle the piping-hot black pepper gravy generously across the meat and potatoes.

## Notes

- **The Double Dredge:** The dry-wet-dry dipping technique gives cube steak its signature craggy, thick crust that clings to the meat rather than falling off in the pan.
- **Skillet Flavor:** Never wipe out the pan before making the gravy; those browned bits left from frying the steaks are where all the rich flavor lives.
